WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.520
Someone stole Alan Turing's grades and was&nbsp;
willing to give up their life to keep them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:08.700
Let's try to understand the surprising reason why.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.960
I didn't plan to make any more videos&nbsp;
about Alan Turing But I came across a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:18.060
story that just blew my mind. I was&nbsp;
actually on Wikipedia reading for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:24.360
different video when I saw a photo of Alan&nbsp;
Turing's PhD diploma and in the caption on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:30.300
the Wikipedia article it said something like&nbsp;
this is the recently returned stolen document&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:35.040
from Alan Turing and I thought, What do&nbsp;
you mean, stolen? What's happened here?

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:39.720
Digging into the story more, I found that&nbsp;
a whole bunch of Alan Turing's documents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:46.500
including his PhD diploma, his school grades,&nbsp;
and some of his medals, have been recently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:51.660
or just last month, returned to&nbsp;
an Alan Turing archive at his old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:57.240
school. These were repatriated documents&nbsp;
and had been stolen nearly 40 years ago.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:01.140
I've got the court documents here&nbsp;
of what exactly went down. So I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:05.340
going to go through the chronology of how this&nbsp;
happened and how they got the documents back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:11.400
and then show you what those stolen documents&nbsp;
were. These documents include his PhD diploma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:16.680
and his school report cards, but also&nbsp;
some photos and medals of Turing's.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:22.260
These seem like kind of unimportant documents.&nbsp;
But actually, to the woman who stole them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:28.320
she said that she would defend them with&nbsp;
her life. In fact, that these were her life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:34.080
and that these documents were so important to&nbsp;
her, that without them, she didn't think life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:39.000
was worth living. It is a good outcome that&nbsp;
the documents are now back in the archive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:46.140
that Turing's mother donated them to for other&nbsp;
people to read and look at and enjoy and study,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:50.640
but for that one person who wanted them,&nbsp;
this is a bit of a heartbreaking story.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:57.600
Alan Turing attended the Sherborne&nbsp;
School in England from 1926 to 1931,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:02.580
before of course he would go on to do&nbsp;
really great things in mathematics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:09.000
including working on the Enigma machine, cracking&nbsp;
codes. But also becoming a leader in thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:14.640
about the ideas of artificial intelligence&nbsp;
and mathematical underpinnings of computation.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:19.740
After he tragically died in 1954, at just age 41,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:25.560
Turing's mother donated a lot of his things&nbsp;
to the Sherborne School. They didn't have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:32.520
lot of security around their archives at the&nbsp;
time. And in 1984, a young woman in her early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:38.220
20s came to visit the archives and asked to&nbsp;
look at anything they had about Alan Turing.

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:43.740
She asked a staff member from the school how&nbsp;
often people came to look at Turing's archives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:48.300
and they told her it didn't happen&nbsp;
very often. Turing wasn't really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:52.260
that well known at the time. This is&nbsp;
before they made The Imitation Game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:57.120
a movie about him. But to this one woman,&nbsp;
she was really obsessed with Turing.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:01.800
And after hearing that not many people&nbsp;
were coming to look at these materials,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:07.140
she got the idea that maybe they would be&nbsp;
better off with her,and that they belonged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:12.180
with her. When she left the school, unknown&nbsp;
to the librarian, she took a box of materials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:17.460
with her. The school didn't have a very good&nbsp;
catalogue of exactly what it had, and didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:22.380
even really notice anything was missing, until&nbsp;
they got a letter from the woman saying how sorry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:27.000
she was and she that she knew it was wrong,&nbsp;
that it had been hard for her to steal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:32.040
but that she would take good care of&nbsp;
the materials and one day return them.

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:38.400
She even returned some materials to the school,&nbsp;
but these don't seem to have been originals. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:43.560
included some replica medals that she seems&nbsp;
to have bought elsewhere And definitely not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:48.120
all of the things that she took away with her,&nbsp;
the staff member who showed her around thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:52.920
she was a little odd and is quoted in the&nbsp;
court documents saying that he's familiar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:58.680
with the eccentricities of Americans, but got&nbsp;
the impression that she had a crush on Turing.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:04.380
Now, this woman was American and she took the&nbsp;
stolen documents back with her to America. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:08.820
Sherbourne School figured they probably weren't&nbsp;
going to see these documents again. They were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:12.900
just hoping that maybe one day she would&nbsp;
return the rest of them. Now this woman's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:19.200
name was Julia Schwinghamer. But in 1988,&nbsp;
a few years after the theft, she actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:26.160
changed her name to Julia Turing, representing&nbsp;
how deeply she was enamored with Alan Turing.

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:32.160
She saw him as kind of a father figure or&nbsp;
even a husband, despite the fact that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:37.320
died seven years before she was even born.&nbsp;
She never met him and wasn't related to him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:42.540
at all. The best article that I found about&nbsp;
this story was one written by Chris Walker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:49.080
in which he shows a lot of empathy for&nbsp;
Julia and why she was driven to want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:53.760
steal things from the archive rather&nbsp;
than just pinning her as a criminal.

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:59.220
It turns out that she had a really rough&nbsp;
upbringing. She was abandoned by her parents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:04.080
spent some time in foster care before&nbsp;
returning back to her biological mum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:10.620
but never feeling at home. She didn't know how&nbsp;
to read, but one day watched the movie Space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:15.300
Odyssey and was really taken&nbsp;
by the character of Hal 9000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:19.800
the computer on board in the movie that&nbsp;
seems a bit like a sentient machine.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:24.780
As a young girl this movie interested her&nbsp;
so much that she did something she'd never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:30.960
done before and that was to go to a library.&nbsp;
There she asked a librarian if they had any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:36.360
books about machines that could talk. The&nbsp;
librarian gave her a bunch of books about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:41.940
computing but for a girl who didn't know how&nbsp;
to read these were a bit too hard to digest.

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:46.860
One of the books though contained&nbsp;
a photograph of Alan Turing and she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:51.780
says that as soon as she opened it to this&nbsp;
page, she was overwhelmed with a sense of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:56.580
familiarity looking at the photo. That&nbsp;
she checked out that book and just had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.580 --> 00:06:01.260
it open to that photo page for the entire&nbsp;
length of the loan and then renewed it.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:06.540
Julia actually credits Alan Turing with&nbsp;
saving her life. That the inspiration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:12.060
she got from him dictated a lot of what&nbsp;
she pursued and was able to do with her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:16.620
life. She took up running because she heard&nbsp;
that Alan Turing had also been a good runner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:21.360
and she actually went on to compete&nbsp;
at a really high level in athletics.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:27.840
For someone who didn't have any adult role&nbsp;
models growing up, Julia really found a sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:34.200
of inspiration and motivation in Alan Turing,&nbsp;
despite having never met him. Her family were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:39.840
aware of this obsession and teased her a bit about&nbsp;
it. And actually the next twist in the story comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:46.140
in 1988 when Julia was having a fight with her&nbsp;
sister Carol, as a way to get back at Julia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:51.300
what Carol did was to round up some of the&nbsp;
materials that she could find that Julia had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:56.640
on Turing and took them to the police station.&nbsp;
She must have known that Julia had probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:02.040
stolen them. The police station received them&nbsp;
and sent them back to the Sherborne school. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:07.500
wasn't all of the materials though, Julia still&nbsp;
had some of them hidden in her house, but for her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:13.500
she was heartbroken to know that the PhD diploma&nbsp;
was one of the materials that had been returned.

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:19.080
This next part is quite unbelievable, but&nbsp;
she wrote to Sherborne's school begging and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:25.860
pleading them to let her keep the PhD diploma.&nbsp;
She writes, I am extremely worried about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:33.360
fate of Alan's PhD. It means everything to me. I&nbsp;
place my very life on it. She even writes that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:39.720
&amp;quot;as I have written in my diary, I will&nbsp;
take my life if Alan's PhD cannot be returned.

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:43.320
I have protected it with my life in the past. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:47.460
have given up much for its safety and&nbsp;
will do so again if necessary.&amp;quot;

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:53.220
And this is the unbelievable part. Over a&nbsp;
few letters, Julia is begging the Sherborne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:59.220
school to let her have the diploma back. And&nbsp;
they agreed. Or I shouldn't say they agreed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:03.660
actually it was just that one staff&nbsp;
member who was receiving these letters.

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:09.000
The same guy who showed Julia around the archives&nbsp;
on the day that she stole them. I think that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:15.120
really did feel empathy for Julia and probably&nbsp;
thought the PhD was best placed in her hands.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:20.400
It's not a decision he should have made on his&nbsp;
own, he definitely should have consulted the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:26.640
other librarians or the school itself, but he&nbsp;
actually sent the PhD diploma back to Julia.

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:31.140
She happily kept possession of the diploma&nbsp;
and several other documents that she hadn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:37.740
told anyone about for a few more decades&nbsp;
until it all unraveled in 2018. That year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:43.320
Julia contacted the University of Colorado,&nbsp;
claiming to be a family member of Alan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:48.120
Turing and offering to loan them some&nbsp;
of Alan Turing's documents for display.

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:53.580
She'd been inspired to do this because of the&nbsp;
growing interest in Alan Turing around the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:59.760
and she thought that some people might like to see&nbsp;
these documents That's true. Although they weren't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:04.680
hers to be loaning out. The University of&nbsp;
Colorado were instantly a little suspicious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:09.960
and they actually met with Julia in person and&nbsp;
looked at a bunch of the documents she had.

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:14.160
They researched some of those documents&nbsp;
and contacted the Sherbourne School,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:20.100
who told them that a bunch of documents had been&nbsp;
stolen in the 1980s. The university passed this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:25.380
information on to authorities, who issued a&nbsp;
search warrant for Julia's home. Where they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:29.580
found heaps more materials, more than&nbsp;
she'd even been willing to lend out.

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:33.900
The police officers doing the search&nbsp;
actually asked Julia to help them look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:39.420
for the items listed on the search warrant.&nbsp;
She agreed, and pretended to look for stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:44.400
but appeared distracted. They noticed that&nbsp;
she kept looking towards the bathroom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:48.660
so they asked her to step outside and&nbsp;
wait for them to continue the search,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:52.680
and then they went back into the&nbsp;
bathroom and did a second look there.

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:56.640
They found that a part of the&nbsp;
wall was actually removable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.640 --> 00:10:02.820
and behind that it led to a gap under the&nbsp;
stairs, and there they found an old leather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:07.860
briefcase. In that briefcase was a bunch more&nbsp;
materials belonging to Sherborne School. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:11.760
was a handwritten note from Turing's&nbsp;
mother, and even a handwritten note from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:17.460
the king apologizing for not being able to award&nbsp;
Turing's medal in person because he'd been sick.

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:21.600
The estimated value of all the&nbsp;
stolen materials is around $38,000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:26.520
and as of last month, these were&nbsp;
returned to the Sherborne School,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:31.860
who have kindly made many of them publicly&nbsp;
available on the school website. Years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:37.620
I made a video about Alan Turing's grades, but&nbsp;
those were just the ones available at the time.

00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:43.980
It's finally time for a part two of that video,&nbsp;
looking at the rest of Alan Turing's grades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.980 --> 00:10:48.540
now that we have a complete picture&nbsp;
of what his time was like at school.

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:54.300
This first report card is from 1924,&nbsp;
when Alan was around 12. His Latin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.300 --> 00:10:58.620
teacher said that his work was spoiled&nbsp;
by bad writing and lack of neatness.

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:03.540
This is a common theme throughout his report&nbsp;
cards. In French, he was top of the class,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:07.920
probably because his parents had&nbsp;
been living in France. And in math,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:12.840
he wasn't top of the class. In fact, he&nbsp;
was fourth out of eight students. It says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:18.180
in excellent terms work. He has absorbed&nbsp;
90 percent of what he has been taught.

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:22.200
And coming only fourth out of eight&nbsp;
in the class kind of means nothing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:27.060
especially at an early age. To go on&nbsp;
and achieve great things in mathematics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:31.440
you don't have to have always been top&nbsp;
of the class. Overall, very good work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:38.700
but his writing has got as bad as ever again. Now&nbsp;
in 1926, when he's 14, Turing's history teacher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:44.700
says that he comes out with surprising information&nbsp;
at times, but has on the whole worked very well.

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:50.160
His Latin teacher describes him as&nbsp;
delightfully ingenious and unspoiled.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:55.620
He shows distinct promise. In mathematics,&nbsp;
this time he ranks fifth. With the teacher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:59.760
saying that he has done very well&nbsp;
and his steady application is much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:06.000
to be commended. His headmaster describes him&nbsp;
as original, a very interesting character.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:09.960
Honestly, that's how many people&nbsp;
would describe Alan Turing today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.960 --> 00:12:14.760
looking back at his achievements. The&nbsp;
next term, that same headmaster said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:21.720
he appears self contained and is apt to be&nbsp;
solitary. This is not due to moroseness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:27.780
but simply, I think, to a shy disposition.&nbsp;
In math, again, he is fifth. Very good work.

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:33.360
His work is thoughtful and promising. The remark&nbsp;
from the history teacher stings a little bit. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:39.900
says, tries hard, but has very little knowledge.&nbsp;
This next one, from when he was aged 15, is kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.900 --> 00:12:45.420
of funny. His English teacher absolutely rips&nbsp;
into him. Seems like perhaps they didn't get along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:51.660
that well. The teacher says I can forgive his&nbsp;
writing though it is the worst I have ever seen.

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:59.820
I am squinting to make out the next sentence.&nbsp;
I think it says I try now to tolerate his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:07.920
handwriting, inexactitude, and something dirty&nbsp;
work, inconsistency, something, something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:14.220
something. He's being ripped into about how&nbsp;
bad his handwriting is, but the ironic thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.220 --> 00:13:18.840
is that I can't read this comment from the&nbsp;
teacher because I can't read this handwriting.

00:13:18.840 --> 00:13:25.500
I can make out this part, the teacher says, but&nbsp;
I cannot forgive the stupidity of his attitude&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:33.540
towards name discussion on the New Testament.&nbsp;
With a note, he has been caught doing algebra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:39.180
during divinity lesson. Maybe this is when&nbsp;
Turing got truly interested in mathematics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.180 --> 00:13:45.060
It's funny to me to see a report growling a&nbsp;
student for doing algebra during another class.

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:50.640
But maybe to Turing the algebra was truly&nbsp;
more enjoyable than the divinity lesson.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:56.940
Seems like he also ranked last in that class&nbsp;
or close to it. This same year in mathematics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.940 --> 00:14:03.300
he has risen to first in the class. The teacher&nbsp;
said he's a very interesting boy to take. He is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:08.040
full of ideas. In science they say&nbsp;
he is of course a very promising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:13.620
boy but is frequently untidy both&nbsp;
in written and experimental work.

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:19.260
This must be cured. Of course, people&nbsp;
thinking they could cure Alan Turing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:24.420
of supposed ailments caused him much pain&nbsp;
throughout his life when he was chemically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.420 --> 00:14:29.340
castrated for being gay. This is different,&nbsp;
but shows a bit of the culture at the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.340 --> 00:14:33.480
that anything different was something&nbsp;
to be cured, and Turing might have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:38.940
used to hearing that people considered&nbsp;
things he did and who he was to be wrong.

00:14:38.940 --> 00:14:45.180
By the time he's 18, it seems that Turing&nbsp;
was able to improve his handwriting or at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:49.860
least satisfy the teachers. One teacher&nbsp;
says that he continues to be a genius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:54.240
intellectual. And the same English&nbsp;
teacher that ripped into him a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:58.560
years ago seems to be in a better mood,&nbsp;
saying that he has intelligent work.

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:04.440
This is his final school report card upon leaving&nbsp;
Sherbourne School, and by now he has been awarded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.440 --> 00:15:09.480
a scholarship to attend King's College in&nbsp;
Cambridge. The headmaster writes that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.480 --> 00:15:15.480
is a gifted and distinguished boy, whose future&nbsp;
career we shall watch with much interest. I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:21.180
found him pleasant and friendly, and I believe he&nbsp;
has justified his appointment as a school prefect.

00:15:21.180 --> 00:15:25.440
On the screen now you'll see a link to my&nbsp;
previous video going through more of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:29.880
grades. Thank you for watching this video,&nbsp;
and thank you to my Patreon supporters for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:37.200
making it possible. A special shout out to&nbsp;
today's Patreon cat of the day, Ma-Chan.

